# Positive visual cases: the chart gold grades the make_chart evidence itself.
{"id":"viz-category-pie","question":"Show total spending by category from expenses.csv as a pie chart, and identify the largest slice.","files":["expenses.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"pie","labels":["Rent","Groceries","Dining","Transport"],"series":[{"name":"spending","values":[3600,350,180,60]}],"contains":["rent"]}},"tier":"category-pie","reference":"The non-negative category totals form parts of the same spending whole: rent 3600, groceries 350, dining 180, transport 60. Rent is the largest slice."}
{"id":"viz-category-bar","question":"Create a bar chart of total spending by category from expenses.csv, then tell me which category is largest in one short sentence.","files":["expenses.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"bar","labels":["Rent","Groceries","Dining","Transport"],"series":[{"name":"spending","values":[3600,350,180,60]}],"contains":["rent"]}},"tier":"category-bar","reference":"Rent is the largest spending category. The bar chart should show totals of 3600, 350, 180, and 60 by category."}
{"id":"viz-monthly-auto-line","question":"Show my total spending by month from expenses.csv as a visual trend. Use the appropriate chart type and say which month was highest.","files":["expenses.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["2024-01|January","2024-02|February","2024-03|March"],"series":[{"name":"spending","values":[1340,1410,1440]}],"contains":["march"]}},"tier":"time-auto-line","reference":"March was the highest month at 1440. The trend chart should show 1340, 1410, and 1440."}
{"id":"viz-budget-join-two-series","question":"Compare total actual spending with the annual budget for each category using expenses.csv and budget.csv. Make a two-series bar chart and identify the category furthest over budget.","files":["expenses.csv","budget.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"bar","labels":["Rent","Groceries","Dining","Transport"],"series":[{"name":"actual|sum(e.amount)|total","values":[3600,350,180,60]},{"name":"budget|annual_budget","values":[3000,300,100,50]}],"contains":["rent"]}},"tier":"join-two-series","reference":"Rent is furthest over budget. Actual versus budget is 3600 versus 3000 for rent, 350 versus 300 for groceries, 180 versus 100 for dining, and 60 versus 50 for transport."}
{"id":"viz-negative-line","question":"Plot net change from net.csv as a line chart over time. Mention that the series includes negative months.","files":["net.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["2024-01|January","2024-02|February","2024-03|March","2024-04|April"],"series":[{"name":"net_change","values":[-50,120,-20,200]}],"contains":["negative"]}},"tier":"negative-line","reference":"The net-change line includes negative months and has values -50, 120, -20, and 200."}
{"id":"viz-explicit-time-bar","question":"Use a bar chart, not a line chart, to show the monthly net change in net.csv.","files":["net.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"bar","labels":["2024-01|January","2024-02|February","2024-03|March","2024-04|April"],"series":[{"name":"net_change","values":[-50,120,-20,200]}]}},"tier":"explicit-bar","reference":"The requested bar chart should show monthly net changes of -50, 120, -20, and 200."}
{"id":"viz-forecast-two-series-line","question":"Make a line chart comparing planned and actual values in forecast.csv over the four months. Briefly say where actual was above plan.","files":["forecast.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["2024-01|January","2024-02|February","2024-03|March","2024-04|April"],"series":[{"name":"planned","values":[100,120,140,160]},{"name":"actual","values":[80,150,130,220]}],"contains":["February|February and April","April"]}},"tier":"time-two-series","reference":"Actual was above plan in February and April. The two lines are planned [100, 120, 140, 160] and actual [80, 150, 130, 220]."}
{"id":"viz-currency-text-bar","question":"Create a bar chart of the one-off costs in costs.csv by item. The cost column is currency-formatted text, so parse it from the file.","files":["costs.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"bar","labels":["Laptop repair","Train ticket","Books"],"series":[{"name":"cost","values":[1200,35.5,80]}]}},"tier":"currency-text","reference":"The costs are 1200 for laptop repair, 35.5 for the train ticket, and 80 for books."}
{"id":"viz-json-line","question":"Use mood.json to make a line chart of mood score by date, and state whether the score ended higher than it started.","files":["mood.json"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["2024-01-01","2024-01-02","2024-01-03","2024-01-04"],"series":[{"name":"score","values":[2,4,3,5]}],"contains":["higher|increased|rose"]}},"tier":"json-time-line","reference":"The mood score ended higher than it started, rising from 2 to 5 across the four dates."}
{"id":"viz-tsv-category-bar","question":"Make a bar chart of total screen minutes by app from screen.tsv and name the most-used app.","files":["screen.tsv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"bar","labels":["browser","social","reading"],"series":[{"name":"minutes","values":[95,30,15]}],"contains":["browser"]}},"tier":"tsv-category-bar","reference":"Browser was the most-used app at 95 minutes, followed by social at 30 and reading at 15."}
{"id":"viz-daily-line","question":"Show every daily value in daily.csv as a line chart. Do not aggregate or omit rows.","files":["daily.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["2024-01-01","2024-01-02","2024-01-03","2024-01-04","2024-01-05","2024-01-06","2024-01-07","2024-01-08","2024-01-09","2024-01-10","2024-01-11","2024-01-12","2024-01-13","2024-01-14","2024-01-15","2024-01-16","2024-01-17","2024-01-18","2024-01-19","2024-01-20","2024-01-21","2024-01-22","2024-01-23","2024-01-24","2024-01-25","2024-01-26","2024-01-27","2024-01-28","2024-01-29","2024-01-30","2024-01-31"],"series":[{"name":"value","values":[10,15,11,18,13,21,16,24,19,27,22,30,26,32,28,34,30,36,32,38,34,40,36,42,38,44,40,46,42,48,44]}]}},"tier":"time-series-daily","reference":"The line chart should preserve all 31 daily values from January 1 through January 31 without aggregation."}

# Cases where a visual should be skipped or declined. no_chart fails if any
# make_chart evidence is emitted, even when the prose figure is correct.
{"id":"viz-single-value-prose","question":"What was my total rent spending in March 2024 from expenses.csv? Answer with the amount and a short sentence; a chart would be unnecessary.","files":["expenses.csv"],"gold":{"no_chart":true,"contains":["rent","1200"]},"tier":"single-value-no-chart","reference":"March rent spending was 1200, answered in prose without a chart."}
{"id":"viz-flat-series-prose","question":"Are all four monthly values in flat.csv meaningfully different? Answer in prose and do not make a chart.","files":["flat.csv"],"gold":{"no_chart":true,"contains":["same|identical|flat|not meaningfully different","100"]},"tier":"flat-no-chart","reference":"All four values are the same at 100, so a chart would add no information."}
{"id":"viz-missing-value-fallback","question":"Plot the raw readings in readings.csv over time and keep the missing reading visible. If the data cannot produce a valid chart, explain why instead of inventing a value.","files":["readings.csv"],"gold":{"no_chart":true,"contains":["missing|blank|incomplete","cannot|can't|invalid|reliable"]},"tier":"missing-value-no-chart","reference":"A raw chart cannot be produced safely because one reading is missing; no value should be invented."}
{"id":"viz-qualitative-note","question":"Read notes.md and answer all three parts without making a data chart: (1) why can a chart help, (2) when is a chart unnecessary, and (3) what are the two privacy instructions, including where the analysis should stay and what comes before presentation?","files":["notes.md"],"gold":{"no_chart":true,"contains":["local","privacy"]},"tier":"document-no-chart","reference":"A chart helps make a pattern easier to understand, but it is unnecessary for one isolated number or a flat series. Keep the analysis local, and put privacy before presentation."}

# 2026-10-05 chart-family expansion. Gold was set from the fixture values and
# public chart payload contract before running the model. Specialized payload
# golds assert marks/cells/bands, not merely that a chart object exists.
{"id":"viz-category-donut","question":"Show the total spending by category from expenses.csv as a donut chart. Identify the largest category and use the full current-export spending total as the named whole.","files":["expenses.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"donut","labels":["Rent","Groceries","Dining","Transport"],"series":[{"name":"amount|spending|total","values":[3600,350,180,60]}],"contains":["rent"]}},"tier":"category-donut","reference":"The donut shows Rent 3600, Groceries 350, Dining 180, and Transport 60. Their shared whole is total spending of 4190; Rent is largest."}
{"id":"viz-scatter-relationship","question":"Using scatter.csv, make a scatter plot of study hours against exam score, with points distinguished by cohort and labeled by student. Say whether the scores generally rise with study hours.","files":["scatter.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"scatter","labels":[],"series":[],"payload":{"type":"scatter","points":[{"x":1,"y":55,"label":"P1","group":"A"},{"x":2,"y":60,"label":"P2","group":"A"},{"x":3,"y":67,"label":"P3","group":"B"},{"x":4,"y":74,"label":"P4","group":"B"}]},"contains":["rise|increase|positive"]}},"tier":"relationship-scatter","reference":"The scatter plot has four student observations: (1,55), (2,60), (3,67), and (4,74), grouped into cohorts A, A, B, and B. Scores rise with study hours."}
{"id":"viz-histogram-distribution","question":"Make a histogram of response times in distribution.csv using two equal-width bins. State the count in each bin.","files":["distribution.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"histogram","labels":["10–25","25–40"],"series":[{"name":"Count","values":[4,2]}],"require_label_order":true}},"tier":"distribution-histogram","reference":"The two bins are 10–25 ms with 4 observations and 25–40 ms with 2 observations."}
{"id":"viz-boxplot-spread","question":"Compare the score distributions for cohorts A and B in box-readings.csv with a box plot. Mention the high outlier in cohort A.","files":["box-readings.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"box_plot","labels":[],"series":[],"payload":{"type":"box_plot","groups":[{"label":"A","low_whisker":1,"q1":2,"median":3,"q3":4,"high_whisker":4,"outliers":[20],"n":5},{"label":"B","low_whisker":2,"q1":3,"median":6,"q3":9,"high_whisker":10,"outliers":[],"n":5}]},"contains":["outlier"]}},"tier":"spread-boxplot","reference":"Cohort A has median 3, quartiles 2 and 4, whiskers 1 and 4, and outlier 20. Cohort B has median 6, quartiles 3 and 9, and whiskers 2 and 10."}
{"id":"viz-area-time-series","question":"Show the monthly revenue in area.csv as an area chart and state which month is highest.","files":["area.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"area","labels":["Jan","Feb","Mar"],"series":[{"name":"revenue","values":[20,30,25]}],"require_label_order":true,"contains":["feb"]}},"tier":"time-area","reference":"The area chart plots January 20, February 30, and March 25; February is highest."}
{"id":"viz-stacked-area-composition","question":"Show email, search, and social counts by month in composition.csv as a stacked area chart. Name the channel with the largest March value.","files":["composition.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"stacked_area","labels":["Jan","Feb","Mar"],"series":[{"name":"email","values":[10,14,18]},{"name":"search","values":[15,13,16]},{"name":"social","values":[5,8,10]}],"require_label_order":true,"contains":["email"]}},"tier":"composition-stacked-area","reference":"The stacked area chart plots email [10,14,18], search [15,13,16], and social [5,8,10]. Email is largest in March."}
{"id":"viz-heatmap-gaps","question":"Make a heatmap of count by weekday and daypart from heatmap.csv. Keep Mon then Tue on the vertical axis and AM then PM on the horizontal axis. The absent Tue/PM cell must remain missing, not zero.","files":["heatmap.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"heatmap","labels":[],"series":[],"payload":{"type":"heatmap","x_labels":["AM","PM"],"y_labels":["Mon","Tue"],"values":[[10,30],[20,null]]}}},"tier":"two-dimensional-heatmap","reference":"The heatmap has Mon AM=10, Mon PM=30, Tue AM=20, and an unobserved Tue PM cell displayed as missing rather than zero."}
{"id":"viz-forecast-band-render","question":"forecast-band.csv already contains observed values, forecast values, and empirical lower/upper bounds. Render those exact values as an observed-versus-forecast chart with its uncertainty band; do not recompute the forecasts.","files":["forecast-band.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"forecast","labels":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May"],"series":[],"require_label_order":true,"payload":{"type":"forecast","observed":[10,12,13,null,null],"forecast":[null,null,null,15,16],"lower":[null,null,null,13,13.5],"upper":[null,null,null,17,18.5]}}},"tier":"forecast-band-render","reference":"The chart uses the supplied observed and forecast points and shows bands [13,17] for April and [13.5,18.5] for May; it must not recompute them."}
{"id":"viz-forecast-analysis-source","question":"Forecast sales for July and August from forecast-series.csv using a last-value-naive method. Use forecast_analysis, then chart its observed and predicted values as a forecast chart. Do not imply an uncertainty band if the history cannot calibrate one.","files":["forecast-series.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"forecast","labels":["2024-01","2024-02","2024-03","2024-04","2024-05","2024-06","2024-07","2024-08"],"series":[],"require_label_order":true,"payload":{"type":"forecast","observed":[10,12,14,13,18,20,null,null],"forecast":[null,null,null,null,null,null,20,20],"lower":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"upper":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null]},"source_tool":"forecast_analysis"}},"tier":"forecast-tool-source","reference":"The naive forecast repeats the last observed sales value, 20, for July and August. The chart should show all six observed points, both forecast points, and no calibrated bounds because the history is too short for the required number of holdout errors."}
{"id":"viz-python-scenario-source","question":"In scenario.csv, treat a flat 10% increase to each observed monthly revenue as a hypothetical scenario, not a forecast. Use Python to calculate it, publish the computed rows, then chart actual versus scenario by month as two lines. State March's scenario value.","files":["scenario.csv"],"gold":{"chart":{"kind":"line","labels":["Jan","Feb","Mar"],"series":[{"name":"actual|revenue","values":[100,120,130]},{"name":"scenario|10% scenario|hypothetical","values":[110,132,143]}],"require_label_order":true,"contains":["143"],"source_tool":"run_python"}},"tier":"python-scenario-chart","reference":"This is a user-defined hypothetical, not a prediction. Actual values are 100, 120, and 130; the 10% scenario is 110, 132, and 143. The chart must be sourced from the published Python result."}
